Imports:
  - Types:
      - StateType
      - Action
      - Reducer
      - Store
    From: BrowserKit/Sources/Redux

Usages:
  component_registry: |
    A screen registers itself into the global state (and unregisters on dismissal) by
    dispatching a ComponentAction with an addComponent/removeComponent action type, tagged with
    its AppComponent case and windowUUID. Only registered components have their per-screen
    reducer run at all, so a screen must register before it can respond to any other action.
  one_store_per_process: |
    There is exactly one app-wide store built from this cell's AppState reducer plus a fixed
    list of per-feature middlewares (one entry per screen/feature area, e.g. toolbar, homepage,
    tabs tray, tracking protection). Every screen subscribes to this one store rather than
    owning independent state.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the composition root of the app's Redux state tree: `AppState` is the single
  root state type held by the app-wide `Store` (from the Redux cell), `PresentedComponentsState`
  /`ComponentState` track which per-screen states currently exist (screens can be presented more
  than once, once per window on iPad), `ComponentAction` is how a screen registers/unregisters
  itself, and `ScreenState` is the contract every per-screen state (owned by that screen's own
  feature module elsewhere in the app, not by this cell) must satisfy to participate.

  Use `component_registry` and `one_store_per_process` for how a new screen plugs into this
  tree.

---

"StateType::ScreenState()":
  location: ScreenState.swift
  annotations: |
    Contract every per-screen state must satisfy to be stored inside `ComponentState` and
    retrieved via `AppState`'s component lookup.
  properties:
    "windowUUID -> WindowUUID": |
      Window this screen state belongs to, so the same screen type can exist once per window on
      iPad.

"Action::ComponentAction(windowUUID: WindowUUID, actionType: ActionType, component: AppComponent)":
  location: ComponentAction.swift
  annotations: |
    Registers or unregisters one screen's state in `PresentedComponentsState`.

    `windowUUID`: window the component is being added to or removed from
    `actionType`: an addComponent or removeComponent action type
    `component`: which screen (e.g. homepage, toolbar, tabsTray) this action targets

    Use `component_registry` for when to dispatch this.

"ComponentState()":
  location: PresentedComponentsState.swift
  annotations: |
    Type-erasing wrapper enum holding exactly one currently-presented screen's `ScreenState`
    value per case (e.g. a homepage case wrapping HomepageState, a toolbar case wrapping
    ToolbarState); one case exists per AppComponent. Exposes a reducer (shaped like the
    `Reducer` routine from the Redux cell) that dispatches to the wrapped screen state's own
    reducer.
  properties:
    "associatedAppComponent -> AppComponent": |
      Which AppComponent this case corresponds to.
    "windowUUID -> WindowUUID?": |
      The wrapped screen state's window, if any.

"PresentedComponentsState()":
  location: PresentedComponentsState.swift
  annotations: |
    The list of every screen state currently registered in the app, across every open window.
    Exposes a reducer (shaped like the `Reducer` routine from the Redux cell): the legacy half
    adds or removes entries in response to `ComponentAction`, then both halves forward the
    action to every entry's own `ComponentState` reducer.
  properties:
    "components -> [ComponentState]": |
      Every currently-registered screen state.

"StateType::AppState()":
  location: AppState.swift
  annotations: |
    The single root state type held by the app-wide `Store`. Exposes a reducer (shaped like the
    `Reducer` routine from the Redux cell) that simply forwards to `PresentedComponentsState`'s
    own reducer.

    Use `one_store_per_process` for how the global store is assembled from this type.
  methods:
    "componentState(type: S.Type, for component: AppComponent, window: WindowUUID?) -> state:S?": |
      Look up one screen's typed state.

      `type`: expected concrete `ScreenState` type
      `component`: which AppComponent to look under
      `window`: when given, only a state whose windowUUID matches is returned; when not given,
      the first match (regardless of window) is returned
      `state`: the matching screen state, or nil if none is registered
  properties:
    "presentedComponents -> PresentedComponentsState": |
      Every currently-registered screen state, across every window.

